<p>Ask platform teams what frustrates them about OpenTelemetry and stability comes up quickly: components at different maturity levels, attribute names that change between releases, and upgrades that quietly break dashboards. The project has heard the same thing. In November 2025 its Governance Committee wrote that complexity and lack of stability were creating impediments to production deployments, and set out a plan to change priorities<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>A year on, the first concrete results are in, and Kubernetes telemetry is where most platform teams will feel them.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What 'stable by default' means</h2>
<p>The committee's goals were explicit<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>:</p>
<ul>
<li>All OpenTelemetry distributions should be <strong>stable by default</strong>, with standard mechanisms to opt in to experimental features.</li>
<li>A <strong>single, consistent set of stability criteria</strong>, including documentation, performance testing and benchmarks.</li>
<li>Easier stabilisation of instrumentation libraries, and federation of semantic conventions.</li>
<li><strong>Epoch releases</strong> that are easier for organisations to consume.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a platform team, the practical promise is fewer surprises: stable components you can rely on, and clearly marked opt-ins for anything that isn't.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The Kubernetes example</h2>
<p>The Kubernetes attributes processor, which enriches telemetry with metadata such as pod, namespace and node, shows what that journey involves. Its stability depended on the Kubernetes semantic conventions it emits, so the two efforts moved together<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>:</p>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Nov 2025</td><td>The Kubernetes semantic conventions group began focused work on stabilising Kubernetes attributes, in step with the Collector's stability goals<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Mar 2026</td><td>Kubernetes attributes reached release candidate; the processor offered feature gates to try the new schema<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Jun 2026</td><td>The attributes shipped as stable in Semantic Conventions v1.42.0<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Sep 2026</td><td>The Kubernetes attributes processor reached v1.0.0, meeting the Collector's stable criteria<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The v1.0.0 announcement is clear that moving to the new convention names means <strong>some breakage for existing users</strong>, and points to a migration guide<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. During the transition the processor provided feature gates, <code>processor.k8sattributes.EmitV1K8sConventions</code> and <code>processor.k8sattributes.DontEmitV0K8sConventions</code>, so teams could emit the new attributes before dropping the old ones<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Stability is a one-time cost paid up front: a final round of renames, then a long period where names stop moving. It's worth paying deliberately rather than discovering it in a broken dashboard.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why attribute renames hurt more than they look</h2>
<p>Kubernetes attributes are everywhere in an observability estate: dashboard filters, alert rules, service level objectives, cost allocation by namespace, retention policies, access controls and the queries engineers keep in runbooks. A renamed attribute doesn't throw an error. It just makes a filter match nothing, so a dashboard goes quiet and an alert never fires.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The migration plan I use</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Inventory dependencies on attribute names.</strong> Export dashboards, alert rules, recording rules and saved queries, and search them for the Kubernetes attributes in your telemetry. Include cost reports that group by namespace or workload, as in <a href="/insights/kubernetes-finops-scale-to-zero/">Kubernetes FinOps</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Read the migration guide for your exact version</strong> of the Collector and processor<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>, and list old and new names side by side.</li>
<li><strong>Emit both, then switch.</strong> Where your version supports it, emit the new conventions alongside the old in a non-production environment, update dashboards and alerts to the new names, then stop emitting the old ones.</li>
<li><strong>Use the gateway layer</strong> to manage the transition centrally rather than on every node, which is one reason I favour the agent-to-gateway pattern in <a href="/insights/opentelemetry-collector-deployment-patterns/">Collector deployment patterns</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Add canary checks</strong>: alerts that fire when a key dashboard query returns no data, so silent breakage is caught.</li>
<li><strong>Pin Collector versions</strong> and upgrade deliberately, with a changelog review for every component you use.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>A broader stability strategy</h2>
<p>The Kubernetes processor is one component among many working towards stability<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The same pattern applies elsewhere, from the <a href="/insights/opentelemetry-span-events-deprecation/">span events deprecation</a> to GenAI conventions that are still evolving, covered in <a href="/insights/llm-observability-opentelemetry-genai/">LLM observability</a>. My rules for platform teams:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Prefer stable components</strong> in production, and record each experimental component you depend on with the reason and an owner.</li>
<li><strong>Treat telemetry schemas as interfaces</strong>, with the same versioning discipline as APIs and the contract thinking in <a href="/insights/data-contracts-playbook/">data contracts</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Budget a small, regular amount of time</strong> for observability upgrades, rather than a painful catch-up every two years.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I can help</h2>
